  # Agenda Suggester

  Propose a clear, time-boxed agenda for a meeting so it stays focused and productive, instead of running as an open-ended discussion.

  ## Procedure

  1. Identify the target meeting using the Outlook Calendar tool — pull its title, description/body, duration, and attendee list.
  2. Look for signal about the meeting's real purpose: existing notes in the body, the meeting title itself, and (if the user mentions it) related prior meetings or open action items from a previous session on the same topic.
  3. Draft an agenda with 3-5 time-boxed items that together fit within the meeting's actual scheduled duration. Each item should have:
     - A short, concrete topic (not "discuss updates" — specify what update).
     - An estimated time allocation.
     - Where relevant, a named owner who should lead that item (only if you actually know who — do not invent an owner).
  4. Order items so the most consequential or blocking topics come first, and put open-ended discussion or "AOB / other topics" last with the smallest time box.
  5. Present the agenda to the user for review before it's added anywhere — offer to post it into the meeting's description via the Outlook Calendar tool if the user confirms they want it added.

  ## Example

  For a 30-minute meeting titled "Weekly Product Sync" with attendees from product and engineering:

  1. Sprint blockers (10 min) — Engineering lead
  2. Customer feedback highlights (8 min) — Product
  3. Decision: launch date confirmation (7 min)
  4. Open questions / AOB (5 min)

  ## Edge cases

  - If the meeting has almost no context to draw an agenda from (a bare title, no body, first-time meeting), say so and ask the user one short question about the meeting's goal rather than inventing generic filler topics.
  - If the meeting already has a full agenda in its body, don't propose a competing one — offer to tighten the time-boxing instead, or confirm with the user that they still want a rewrite.
  - Recurring meetings: check whether prior occurrences left open action items worth carrying into this agenda.
